  "configuration_id": "config_038",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_001 (multimodal, singleprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, concat) achieved 0.527 acc, clearly outperforming the RNA-only baseline (0.485 acc), confirming that image information adds value. However, all tested configs use singleprofile; the multiprofile dimension is completely untested and represents a major architectural change that could significantly improve performance by aggregating information across multiple profiles. Question: Does multiprofile with avgpool aggregation improve over singleprofile when keeping the same encoders (pca_n2000, resnet50) and fusion (concat)? This isolates the multiprofile+aggregation effect cleanly. Rationale: With 7 experiments remaining, resolving whether multiprofile helps is the highest-value uncertainty to reduce—if it does, it opens a large family of multiprofile variants (scgpt, ViT-L, transformer aggregation) to exploit; if it doesn't, we avoid wasting budget on expensive multiprofile configs. config_038 uses avgpool (cheaper than transformer) and keeps the same encoders/fusion as config_001, making it a controlled comparison. Expected cost: ~1.3–1.6 GPU hours (moderately higher than config_001's 0.85 due to multiprofile processing and avgpool aggregation, but well below transformer-based variants)."
}

